   "snippet": "A 4 week old firstborn boy has 1 week of progressive nonbilious projectile vomiting and remains hungry after emesis. Examination reveals visible gastric peristalsis and a firm 'olive like' mass in the epigastrium. Laboratory studies show a ",
   "text": "Pediatrics Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis pyloric-stenosis metabolic-alkalosis paradoxical-aciduria pyloromyotomy USMLE-style / MedKOS (pediatric surgery) A 4 week old firstborn boy has 1 week of progressive nonbilious projectile vomiting and remains hungry after emesis. Examination reveals visible gastric peristalsis and a firm 'olive like' mass in the epigastrium. Laboratory studies show a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxically acidic urine; ultrasound confirms a thickened, elongated pylorus. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step? A. Correct the fluid and electrolyte abnormalities with IV normal saline plus potassium, then perform pyloromyotomy B. Proceed to emergent pyloromyotomy before fluid resuscitation C. Start a prokinetic agent and discharge home D. Obtain a barium enema E. Begin broad spectrum intravenous antibiotics 비담즙성 사출성 구토 + 올리브 종괴 + 저염소·저칼륨 대사성 알칼리증·역설적 산성뇨는 비후성 유문협착이다. 응급 수술이 아니라 먼저 수액·전해질(생리식염수+KCl)을 교정한 뒤 유문근절개술(pyloromyotomy)을 한다."
